+# Development performance strategy (Claude): Task E
+
+The GPU is unknown and nothing has been measured, so this document makes no FPS claims. Epic recommends
+64 GB RAM, a 12-core CPU and an SSD for City Sample. The owner has 32 GB, so **memory is the first
+constraint**, ahead of GPU.
+
+## Memory (32 GB)
+
+- Use the smallest playable map. Never "Load All" World Partition cells in the editor. Load a region
+  around the test district only.
+- Keep the project, Derived Data Cache and Windows page file on an SSD. A system-managed page file, or
+  a fixed one of about 32–64 GB, prevents editor OOM crashes during shader compilation and cooking.
+- Close browsers and other launchers while compiling shaders. If compiles exhaust RAM, lower shader
+  workers in the local `Saved/Config` (`[DevOptions.Shaders] NumUnusedShaderCompilingThreads`).
+- The first open compiles a very large number of shaders. Expect a long first session. That is not a
+  failure.
+
+## Runtime presets
+
+`claude_tools/cr_perf_presets.py` applies runtime-only console variables (nothing goes into config).
+Use `--emit low|medium|high` to print them, or `apply()` in Editor Python. All presets keep Lumen
+(software), Nanite, Virtual Shadow Maps and TSR, which City Sample is built around. Disabling them
+changes the look and may break content assumptions, so scale resolution and quality groups instead.
+
+| Lever | Low | Medium | High | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Internal resolution (`r.ScreenPercentage`, TSR) | 50 | 67 | 100 | Biggest GPU lever. TSR upscales. |
+| Lumen GI/reflections (`sg.GlobalIlluminationQuality`, `sg.ReflectionQuality`) | 1 | 2 | 3 | Hardware RT off below High. |
+| Virtual Shadow Maps (`sg.ShadowQuality`) | 1 | 2 | 3 | VSM stays on; quality scales cost. |
+| Nanite detail (`r.Nanite.MaxPixelsPerEdge`) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Higher value means coarser triangles. |
+| Texture pool (`r.Streaming.PoolSize`, MB) | 2000 | 3000 | 4000 | Match to GPU VRAM. Check `stat streaming` for over-budget. |
+| View distance / foliage | 1 | 2 | 3 | Also reduces World Partition streaming pressure. |
+| Frame cap (`t.MaxFPS`) | 60 | 60 | uncapped | Caps keep thermals and measurements stable. |
+
+Crowd and traffic density are **sample-specific** controls: spawner counts, entity configs, and any
+City Sample console variables or settings. Find them locally (spawner Details, `cs_project_scan`
+name hits, the sample's settings menu) before scripting them. For a first-playable build, reduce
+density through the spawners' own count fields in a separate data layer or config. Do not delete
+spawners. After any density change, re-run the preservation diagnostic.
+
+## Profiling procedure
+
+1. Use a fixed spot and fixed time of day, with the same preset and PIE in a standalone window (or a
+   Development build, which is more representative).
+2. `cr_perf_presets.py --emit profile` gives `stat unit`, `stat gpu`, `stat streaming`, `stat memory`
+   and `memreport -full`. Record Game/Draw/GPU ms, the GPU top passes (Lumen, VSM, Nanite, TSR) and
+   streaming over-budget.
+3. Drive the same 2-minute route and capture with Unreal Insights (`-trace=cpu,gpu,frame,memory`)
+   to find hitches: WP cell loads, shader compiles, Mass spawning.
+4. Write results to this file: GPU model, VRAM, driver, preset, ms per frame and the top three costs.
+   Only then choose the default preset.
